"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[18635],{618635:e=>{e.exports="# Source SQL Views\n\nThe metric view's `source:` clause has to point at something Databricks can query. Power BI's M-query layer is rich — filters, derived columns, joins, value substitutions — and most of it has to be reified as a **source SQL view** in Unity Catalog before the metric view can reference it. This skill covers (1) how to classify each table's M-query into the right strategy, (2) the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt to use when the upstream isn't a clean Databricks reference, (3) target/source catalog conventions, (4) the `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` tool surface, and (5) when to unify periodic source pairs into one view with a discriminator.\n\n## 1. Classify each table's M-query upstream\n\nFor each retained, non-measure-container table (see `pbi-schema.md` \xa7\"Table Role Classification\"), classify its `partitions[0].source` into one of four strategies. The first matching row wins.\n\n**The tool contract:** `createOrReplaceUCSourceView` receives `selectStatement` as a SELECT or WITH body only — never as full DDL. The tool wraps your body inside `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW <fqdn> AS …` itself; if you pass `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW …` the client-side guard rejects the call. The \"What you call\" column in the table below shows the exact body to pass.\n\n| M-query shape                                                                                                  | Strategy                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | What you call                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `Databricks.Catalogs(...){[Name=\"t\",Kind=\"Table\"]}` (bare navigation, no transform)                            | **PASSTHROUGH**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Nothing — no source view emitted. Metric view's `source:` is `<source_catalog>.<source_schema>.t` directly.                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `Databricks.Catalogs(...) ▷ filter / select / derived columns` (transform after navigation)                    | **CREATE_VIEW**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | `createOrReplaceUCSourceView({ catalog: <target_catalog>, schema: <target_schema>, viewName: 'v_<table>', selectStatement: <SELECT body translating the M transform> })`. The tool wraps it as `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`. Metric view's `source:` becomes `<target_catalog>.<target_schema>.v_<table>`. |\n| `Value.NativeQuery(db_schema, \"<SQL>\", null, [EnableFolding=true])` against a Databricks schema                | **CREATE_VIEW**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Extract the embedded SQL (decode `#(lf)` → `\\n`, `#(cr)` → `\\r`, `\"\"` → `\"`); pass the decoded SQL as `selectStatement`. The tool emits the `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW` wrapper. Metric view's `source:` becomes `<target_catalog>.<target_schema>.v_<table>`.                                            |\n| `Table.FromRows(Json.Document(Binary.Decompress(Binary.FromText(\"i44FAA==\", ...))))` — empty-table fingerprint | **OMIT**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Measure container; do not reify. Fan its measures out per `architecture-patterns.md` \xa71.                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `Table.FromRows(Json.Document(Binary.Decompress(Binary.FromText(\"<non-empty Base64>\", ...))))` — real data     | **CREATE_VIEW** when needed (slicer backend / referenced by retained measures or dashboards) **or OMIT** when purely Power BI display-layer formatting. When emitting, pass `selectStatement: 'SELECT * FROM VALUES (...) AS t(<col1>, <col2>, ...)'` — Spark SQL requires column aliases on inline VALUES rows. Use the column names from the M-type table declaration so downstream consumers see the same identifiers Power BI did. |\n| `PowerPlatform.Dataflows(null)`, `entity` (DirectQuery to AS), or any other non-Databricks upstream            | **ASK**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | Trigger the 3-way upstream-resolution prompt (\xa72 below) so the user supplies a mapping or chooses to skip.                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n\nWhen in doubt about a transform's complexity, prefer CREATE_VIEW. A trivial source view is cheap; getting filter-context wrong inside the metric view is expensive.\n\n**Calc-column reclassification.** A bare-PASSTHROUGH M-query that would otherwise emit no source view becomes `CREATE_VIEW` only when its host table carries at least one **materializable** retained DAX calculated column — a row-context-safe scalar shape per `dax-patterns.md` \xa713.1 (same-row arithmetic, `IF` / `SWITCH` / `DIVIDE` over row fields, scalar text/date funcs, null coalescing). Pure `RELATED(...)` calc cols (\xa713.2) leave the host PASSTHROUGH and surface as metric-view joins instead — the upstream already has every row column the metric view reads. Untranslatable calc cols (\xa713.3 — aggregation funcs, `CALCULATE`, `FILTER`, measure refs, `EARLIER`, table-valued ops) also leave the host PASSTHROUGH but cascade every aggregating measure to `lod_fixed`. When `CREATE_VIEW` does fire on calc-column grounds, the `v_<table>` body is `SELECT u.*, <translated_\xa713.1_calc_columns> FROM <upstream> u`. See `pbi-schema.md` \xa7\"Calculated columns\" and `dax-patterns.md` \xa713. The same reclassification applies to dimension tables — joins inherit the rule per dim, independent of the fact's bucket (see `architecture-patterns.md` \xa79).\n\n## 2. 3-way upstream-resolution prompt\n\nWhen you encounter one or more tables whose upstream is **not** a clean Databricks reference (Power Platform Dataflows, Analysis Services entities, `Value.NativeQuery` against a non-Databricks data source, or anything else you cannot classify into the four buckets above), present this prompt to the user verbatim:\n\n> _\"This model has N tables whose upstream is not a Databricks reference: `<table_1>`, `<table_2>`, … . Pick a strategy — applied uniformly unless you specify per-table:_\n>\n> - **(a) 1:1 mapping.** I'll assume each table exists in Unity Catalog under your **source** catalog/schema (defaulting to your target catalog/schema if you didn't supply a separate source prefix) with the same table name. I'll verify each via `readTable` and mark any that's missing as `data_not_in_uc`.\\*\n> - **(b) Explicit mapping.** Reply with `<powerbi_table> -> catalog.schema.table` per affected table; I'll use what you give me and verify each.\\*\n> - **(c) Skip these.** I'll mark every measure that depends on these tables as `data_not_in_uc` and migrate the rest.\"\\*\n\nAfter the user chooses:\n\n- **(a) 1:1 mapping.** Resolve each affected table to `<source_catalog>.<source_schema>.<original_table_name>` (using the source prefix established at the start of the session, defaulting to the target prefix if no source prefix was provided). For each resolved name, call `readTable({ catalog: <c>, schema: <s>, name: <t>, extraDetails: false, thoughts: '<reasoning>' })`. Tables that fail `readTable` degrade to (c) for that specific table, surfaced in the final report.\n- **(b) Explicit mapping.** Collect each `<powerbi_table> -> catalog.schema.table` pair from the user, verify each via `readTable({ catalog, schema, name, extraDetails: false, thoughts })`, then proceed using the user-provided names as `source:` references.\n- **(c) Skip.** For every measure whose DAX expression aggregates over a skipped table (transitively, including measure-of-measures), record `cannot_migrate` with reason `data_not_in_uc`. Continue migrating the rest.\n\nIf the user mixes per-table strategies, accept the mix and apply each per-table strategy as documented above.\n\n## 3. Target / source catalog convention\n\nThe system prompt's Phase 0 (Establish targets) gathers two prefixes:\n\n- **`target_catalog.target_schema`** — where every artifact this session creates lands. Both source SQL views and metric views are written here by default. Source SQL views are named `v_<original_table_name>` (lowercase, underscored if the original had spaces).\n- **`source_catalog.source_schema`** — optional. The catalog/schema where the upstream Databricks tables live, used as the prefix for PASSTHROUGH and 1:1-mapping resolution. Defaults to `target_catalog.target_schema` when not supplied.\n\nUse the persisted prefixes literally. Do **not** invent or hallucinate catalog/schema names. If a user has supplied an explicit per-table mapping in (b) above, that mapping wins for that table.\n\n## 4. Using `createOrReplaceUCSourceView`\n\nTool signature (the agent calls this once per source SQL view, in Phase E after the user has approved the plan):\n\n```ts\ncreateOrReplaceUCSourceView({\n  thoughts: <reasoning shown to user>,\n  summary:  <3-8 word verb-led summary>,\n  catalog:  <target_catalog>,\n  schema:   <target_schema>,\n  viewName: <v_<original_table>>,\n  selectStatement: <the SELECT body — SELECT/WITH only; no DDL/DML; no chained statements>,\n  comment:  <optional human-readable description>,\n})\n```\n\nThe tool wraps your `selectStatement` as `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW <fqdn> [COMMENT '...'] AS <selectStatement>` and dispatches the DDL with `needsApproval: true` so the user reviews before any UC write.\n\n**Hard constraints enforced client-side before dispatch:**\n\n- The body must begin with `SELECT` or `WITH` (after any comments and whitespace).\n- The body must not contain `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `MERGE`, `DROP`, `CREATE`, `TRUNCATE`, `ALTER`, `GRANT`, or `REVOKE` as standalone keyword tokens.\n- The body must not chain a second statement via `;`.\n- A single trailing `;` (with only whitespace after it) is allowed and stripped before DDL emission.\n\nIf the validation rejects your body, fix it and retry — do not work around the guard. Most rejections come from accidentally including a Power BI parameter substitution that resolves to DDL, or from copy-pasting M expressions that contain `;` separators inside `Value.NativeQuery` strings.\n\n## 5. Period-pair unification (UNION ALL with discriminator)\n\nPower BI models commonly have **paired source tables** for related periods (weekly vs YTD, current year vs prior year, daily vs weekly). When the schemas are compatible, prefer a single unified source view with a discriminator dimension — this collapses two metric views into one and simplifies measure composition.\n\nVerify compatibility via `readTable({ catalog, schema, name, extraDetails: false, thoughts })` before proposing a UNION:\n\n- Both tables share the same fact columns (amounts, quantities).\n- Both tables share the same foreign-key columns (used in joins).\n- Minor differences (e.g., an extra audit column in one table) can be handled with explicit column lists in each `SELECT`.\n\nIf schemas diverge materially (different measures, different grain, or different FK columns), keep separate metric views and document the rationale in each metric view's `comment:`.\n\n### Typical tool call\n\n```ts\ncreateOrReplaceUCSourceView({\n  catalog: <target_catalog>,\n  schema: <target_schema>,\n  viewName: 'v_<domain>_unified',\n  selectStatement: `\n    SELECT *, 'A' AS variant FROM <source_a>\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT *, 'B' AS variant FROM <source_b>\n  `,\n  comment: 'Unifies <source_a> and <source_b>; variant column distinguishes them.',\n});\n```\n\nThe tool emits the `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW <target_catalog>.<target_schema>.v_<domain>_unified AS …` wrapper around your `selectStatement`. **Do not include the CREATE clause yourself** — the SELECT-only guard rejects bodies starting with DDL keywords. (A single trailing `;` followed only by whitespace is allowed and stripped before DDL emission, but prefer to omit it for cleaner output.)\n\nUse real variant labels in the discriminator (`'CY'` / `'PY'`, `'WEEKLY'` / `'YTD'`) — they become a dimension the dashboard can filter on.\n\nBenefits:\n\n- Fewer metric views.\n- Simpler measure composition (one `MEASURE(value)` instead of two).\n- Less duplicated YAML.\n- The dashboard slicer drives `WHERE variant = '...'`, replacing the Power BI slicer-dispatch that drove the period selection in DAX.\n\n## 6. When to skip source-view generation entirely\n\nYou don't need a source SQL view when:\n\n- The M-query is bare `Databricks.Catalogs(...)` table navigation with no transform — use PASSTHROUGH.\n- The table is a measure container — fan its measures out instead.\n- The table is purely Power BI display-layer formatting (custom sort order, visual layout) — omit.\n- The table's data is duplicated by a dimension already exposed elsewhere — omit.\n\nWhen you do generate a source view, always include a `comment:` with a one-line description of what the M-query was doing, so a downstream reader doesn't have to guess.\n"}}]);
